      }
    },
    {
      "source_number": "734",
      "independent_solution": "Use u^2-v^2=(u-v)(u+v). The six ordered (u,v) pairs are (x,3),(a,sqrt(3)),(2a,sqrt(7)),(4b,sqrt(11)),(sqrt(c),2),(sqrt(5),sqrt(a)). Thus x^2-9=x^2-3^2=(x-3)(x+3); a^2-3=a^2-(sqrt(3))^2=(a-sqrt(3))(a+sqrt(3)); 4a^2-7=(2a)^2-(sqrt(7))^2=(2a-sqrt(7))(2a+sqrt(7)); 16b^2-11=(4b)^2-(sqrt(11))^2=(4b-sqrt(11))(4b+sqrt(11)); c-4=(sqrt(c))^2-2^2=(sqrt(c)-2)(sqrt(c)+2) with the original c>0; 5-a=(sqrt(5))^2-(sqrt(a))^2=(sqrt(5)-sqrt(a))(sqrt(5)+sqrt(a)) with the original a>0. The first four are real-variable identities. The last condition does not require a<5; factor order is not unique.",
      "coverage": {
        "verdict": "pass",
        "evidence": "n31-difference-of-squares includes a complete square-difference and factorization chain for each of the six source expressions. The c>0 and a>0 conditions are local to the original last two parts, and the first four remain real-variable identities. Both transformation stages are judged together. Independent construction and equivalent factor-order enumeration are declared losses."
      }
    },
    {
      "source_number": "735",
      "independent_solution": "Factoring and cancelling the actual nonzero denominator gives, in order, a-2 with a!=-2; c+sqrt(6) with c!=sqrt(6); sqrt(x)-sqrt(2) with x>=0, since sqrt(x)+sqrt(2)>0; sqrt(a)+sqrt(b) with a,b>=0 and a!=b, since sqrt(a)-sqrt(b)!=0 iff a!=b on nonnegative reals. These are independent subpart domains, not their intersection. x=0 and x=2 remain allowed in the third part. Cancellation does not restore a=-2,c=sqrt(6) or a=b in the relevant original fractions.",
